package teyru

/* java.util.zip's archive layer: ZipEntry, ZipOutputStream, ZipInputStream and
   ZipFile, over the Deflater, Inflater and CRC32 in lib/44_zip.teyru.

   A ZIP archive is a container, not a compression format, and that is what
   makes it a different problem from the gzip in 44_zip: the bytes a reader
   needs are in three places at once. Each entry has a local file header and its
   data; every entry is described again in the central directory at the end of
   the archive, together with the offset of its local header; and the central
   directory is found through the end-of-central-directory record, which is the
   last thing written and the only record whose position is fixed -- a reader
   that cannot seek has to walk the local headers, and a reader that can seek
   goes to the end first. ZipInputStream is the walker, ZipFile is the seeker,
   and they share the field layout in this file rather than each having one.

   What is here. ZipEntry is the record both of them produce: the name, the
   size, the compressed size, the CRC-32, the compression method, the MS-DOS
   date and time, the extra field and the comment. ZipOutputStream writes an
   archive -- STORED and DEFLATED entries, the local headers, the data
   descriptors when they are needed, the central directory and the end record --
   and ZipInputStream and ZipFile read one back. ZipFile additionally answers
   for an entry by name and for one entry's data at a time, which is what a
   random-access reader is for.

   The format decisions, and why each is a decision rather than a default.

   * A local header is written ONCE, before the entry's data, and whether it
     carries the sizes is decided at that moment. When the caller has supplied
     the size, the compressed size and the CRC-32 (all three, and for a DEFLATED
     entry the compressed size has to have been set by the caller rather than
     inherited from a reader, which is Java's csizeSet rule), they go into the
     local header and the entry needs nothing after its data. Otherwise the
     header says zero and the three numbers follow the data as a data descriptor
     with the 0x08074b50 signature, and general purpose flag bit 3 says so --
     which is the only way to write an entry whose sizes are not known until it
     has been compressed, and is what the JDK itself does in that case. A STORED
     entry has no such option: a reader has to know where its data ends before
     it reads one byte of it, so its sizes are required up front and this writer
     refuses the entry rather than guessing. The choice is per entry and made by
     the entry's own fields, exactly as in java.util.zip, so the same program
     produces the same structure with both libraries.

   * The general purpose bit flags that are honoured are three. Bit 0
     (encryption) is rejected by both readers with the JDK's message: an
     encrypted entry cannot be read, and reading its bytes as if they were data
     would be a decryption that silently produces the wrong bytes. Bit 3 (data
     descriptor) is set by the writer when the sizes are unknown, and is what
     tells a reader to take the CRC-32, the compressed size and the size from
     after the data rather than from the header. Bit 11 (the language encoding
     flag, EFS) is set on every entry this writer writes, because a Teyru string
     IS its UTF-8 bytes and there is no other charset to write in; a reader
     ignores it for the same reason. The rest -- the level hints in bits 1 and
     2, patched data, strong encryption, the reserved bits -- are read into the
     entry's flag field and not otherwise acted on, which is also what the JDK
     does with them.

   * Names are UTF-8 bytes, always, with bit 11 set to say so, and the writer
     has no charset parameter: java.util.zip's ZipOutputStream(OutputStream,
     Charset) exists because a Java string is UTF-16 and has to choose, and this
     language's strings are already the bytes the format wants. A reader decodes
     a name without bit 11 as UTF-8 too, which is what the JDK's default
     ZipCoder does (the ZIP specification's other answer, CP437, is not
     implemented anywhere in this library); an archive whose names were written
     in CP437 is read as its bytes, which is right for the ASCII names that are
     nearly all of them.

   * ZIP64 is refused rather than half-written. Everything the format needs for
     an archive over 4 GiB, over 65534 entries, or with an entry at an offset
     over 4 GiB, is a second set of 64-bit records: a ZIP64 extra field in both
     the local and central headers, a ZIP64 end-of-central-directory record and
     a locator. Writing the low 32 bits of a value that does not fit would
     produce an archive that reads back truncated, so a value that does not fit
     a field is a ZipException naming the field, and a reader that meets one of
     the magic 0xFFFFFFFF or 0xFFFF values says ZIP64 is not implemented here
     instead of misreading it. An archive small enough for 32-bit fields is what
     this file does; that is every archive this library writes.

   * The extra field is carried, not interpreted. A caller's bytes are written
     into both headers exactly as given, and a reader hands them back exactly as
     it read them. Two extra fields are therefore NOT synthesised: the
     extended-timestamp field (0x5455) that records a unix time beside the DOS
     one, and the ZIP64 field above. That has one visible consequence on the
     reading side: the JDK prefers the extended timestamp, when an entry carries
     one, over the MS-DOS date and time, and this ZipEntry does not -- getTime
     and getTimeLocal answer from the DOS fields, which are the fields the
     format defines and the only ones this library writes.

   * The CRC-32 and the uncompressed size are checked by BOTH readers when an
     entry's data has been read to its end, and the compressed size is checked
     with them. java.util.zip's ZipInputStream does the same; its ZipFile does
     not -- a ZipFile entry stream in the JDK hands back whatever the inflater
     produced without looking at the CRC-32 or the size at all. This is the one
     place this file deliberately disagrees with the JDK, and it disagrees in
     the direction that costs a caller nothing when the archive is intact: a
     corrupt or truncated entry that javac's ZipFile would return silently is a
     ZipException here, with the JDK's own message for it.

   * A time is the MS-DOS date and time, the only timestamp the format has, and
     that part is exact: setTimeLocal and getTimeLocal convert between those
     fields and java.time.LocalDateTime without a zone, which is what the JDK
     does with the same two methods. setTime(long) and getTime() work in epoch
     milliseconds, and they are where this library's one time convention shows:
     there is no zone database anywhere in this library (see the note at the top
     of lib/20_time.teyru), so an instant becomes a date and time in UTC, where
     Java uses the JVM's default zone. They agree with javac exactly when the
     JVM's default zone is UTC and differ by the offset otherwise, which is the
     same deviation every other date in this library has. Nothing about an
     archive depends on the difference: whichever zone wrote the DOS fields is
     not recorded in them.

   Everything else about the shape of the two readers. ZipInputStream reads the
   local headers in order and stops when the signature stops matching, which is
   how it ends at the central directory -- the archive's end is "the next four
   bytes are not a local header", not a record with a length in it. Like
   GZIPInputStream in 44_zip, it reads its input one byte at a time unless the
   stream under it is a ByteArrayInputStream, because this library's InputStream
   declares read() and readln() and no block read, and it keeps the bytes the
   inflater did not consume in a pending region so that the next header starts
   where the data ended rather than where the last read happened to end.
   ZipFile reads the whole archive into memory and parses the central directory
   from its end: this library has no seekable byte channel, and a byte array is
   what a file, a jar and an HTTP body all already are. It answers entries() as
   an Enumeration, as Java does, over a snapshot of the central directory taken
   when the archive is opened.

   How this file is verified. tests/programs/t185_zip_archive.teyru and
   t186_zip_file.teyru are the same programs as the javac 21 programs in their
   .java.ref files, and their .expected files are those programs' output on a
   JVM whose default zone is UTC. Both are interoperable in both directions:
   t185 carries one archive the JDK's ZipOutputStream wrote and one this
   library's, and both programs read both, and t186 writes an archive to a file
   with one library and reads it back with the other's ZipFile. A STORED-only
   archive written here is byte for byte the JDK's for the same entries, which
   is checked directly rather than argued; a DEFLATED one is not, because the
   compressed bytes are the compressor's business (lib/44_zip.teyru says where
   that equality holds and where it does not). The archives this library writes
   are also read by Info-ZIP's unzip, a third implementation, which is what
   settles that the headers are not merely what this library's own reader
   expects. */

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the entry

/* java.util.zip.ZipEntry: one entry of an archive as both sides of this file
   see it.

   The fields are package-level rather than private, exactly as they are in
   java.util.zip, because ZipOutputStream, ZipInputStream and ZipFile set them
   where the JDK's three do -- a decoder fills in the size, the compressed size
   and the CRC-32 of an entry it just read, and the writer fills them in for a
   DEFLATED entry that was written with a data descriptor. The accessors below
   are the public surface, and they are Java's.

   One difference in the surface: java.util.zip.ZipEntry has no accessor at all
   for the general purpose bit flags -- they are package-private, and only the
   three classes beside it read them. There is no package boundary to hide them
   behind here either, so they are a package-level field like the rest, and a
   caller who wants to see what a reader found in a header reads the field. The
   method has Java's own public accessors, and both of them check the value the
   way Java's do. */
class ZipEntry extends Object {
  /* Java's two methods. Any other value is refused by the writer and by both
     readers, which is what java.util.zip does with a method it does not know. */
  public static final int STORED = 0
  public static final int DEFLATED = 8

  /* The MS-DOS date and time this class stores for a timestamp before 1980,
     which is 1980-01-01 00:00:00: the earliest moment the field can express.
     Java clamps to the same value, from the same place. */
  static final long DOSTIME_BEFORE_1980 = (1 << 21) | (1 << 16)

  String name
  /* The MS-DOS date and time as the format packs it, or -1 when nothing has set
     one. Java calls this field xdostime and keeps a fractional second in its
     high half; there is no such half here, so the value is exactly the four
     bytes an archive carries and a millisecond set through setTime is lost, as
     it is in the file. */
  long dosTime = -1
  long size = -1
  long csize = -1
  long crc = -1
  int method = -1
  int flag = 0
  /* Whether the compressed size came from setCompressedSize rather than from a
     reader. ZipOutputStream's rule for whether an entry's sizes can be trusted
     turns on it, as it does in the JDK. */
  boolean csizeSet = false
  byte[] extra = null
  String comment = null

  public ZipEntry(String name) {
    if (name == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("name")
    }
    /* Java's check, on characters rather than on the UTF-8 bytes a header
       carries: whether the bytes fit is the writer's question, and it is asked
       there too. */
    if (name.length() > 0xFFFF) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("entry name too long")
    }
    this.name = name
  }

  /* Java's copy constructor. The extra field is copied rather than shared, so
     that writing one entry's extra data cannot change another's. */
  public ZipEntry(ZipEntry e) {
    if (e == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException()
    }
    name = e.name
    dosTime = e.dosTime
    size = e.size
    csize = e.csize
    crc = e.crc
    method = e.method
    flag = e.flag
    csizeSet = e.csizeSet
    comment = e.comment
    if (e.extra == null) {
      extra = null
    } else {
      extra = e.extra.clone()
    }
  }

  public String getName() {
    return name
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------ the time

  /* An instant, as the MS-DOS date and time in UTC. Java reads the instant in
     the JVM's default zone; the difference is the point of the note at the top
     of this file, and of the one at the top of lib/20_time.teyru.

     A time before 1980 has no spelling in the field and becomes 1980-01-01,
     which is what java.util.zip does with one. */
  public void setTime(long millis) {
    if (millis < 0) {
      dosTime = DOSTIME_BEFORE_1980
      return
    }
    long second = millis / 1000
    long days = second / 86400
    long ofDay = second % 86400
    long[] civil = TimeMath.civilFromDays(days)
    int year = (int) civil[0]
    if (year < 1980) {
      dosTime = DOSTIME_BEFORE_1980
      return
    }
    dosTime = pack(year, (int) civil[1], (int) civil[2],
      (int) (ofDay / 3600), (int) ((ofDay % 3600) / 60), (int) (ofDay % 60))
  }

  /* The instant the DOS fields name, read in UTC. -1 when no time has been set,
     which is Java's answer for an entry that never had one. */
  public long getTime() {
    if (dosTime == -1) {
      return -1
    }
    int year = (int) (((dosTime >> 25) & 0x7f) + 1980)
    int month = (int) ((dosTime >> 21) & 0x0f)
    int day = (int) ((dosTime >> 16) & 0x1f)
    int hour = (int) ((dosTime >> 11) & 0x1f)
    int minute = (int) ((dosTime >> 5) & 0x3f)
    int second = (int) ((dosTime << 1) & 0x3e)
    long days = TimeMath.daysFromCivil(year, month, day)
    return days * 86400000L + (hour * 3600L + minute * 60L + second) * 1000L
  }

  /* The DOS fields as a local date and time, exactly as java.util.zip's
     setTimeLocal does it: no zone is involved in either direction, so this pair
     agrees with the JDK on every machine. */
  public void setTimeLocal(LocalDateTime t) {
    if (t == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("time")
    }
    int year = t.toLocalDate().getYear()
    if (year < 1980) {
      dosTime = DOSTIME_BEFORE_1980
      return
    }
    dosTime = pack(year, t.toLocalDate().getMonthValue(), t.toLocalDate().getDayOfMonth(),
      t.toLocalTime().getHour(), t.toLocalTime().getMinute(), t.toLocalTime().getSecond())
  }

  /* null when the entry has no time, where the JDK's version walks its -1
     through the field layout and raises a DateTimeException for a month of 15. */
  public LocalDateTime getTimeLocal() {
    if (dosTime == -1) {
      return null
    }
    return LocalDateTime.of(
      (int) (((dosTime >> 25) & 0x7f) + 1980),
      (int) ((dosTime >> 21) & 0x0f),
      (int) ((dosTime >> 16) & 0x1f),
      (int) ((dosTime >> 11) & 0x1f),
      (int) ((dosTime >> 5) & 0x3f),
      (int) ((dosTime << 1) & 0x3e))
  }

  /* The MS-DOS date and time field: five bits of year from 1980, four of month,
     five of day, five of hour, six of minute and five of second divided by two.
     Seconds are the reason an archive's timestamps have two-second resolution
     and this class has no milliseconds to lose. */
  static long pack(int year, int month, int day, int hour, int minute, int second) {
    long y = (long) (year - 1980)
    long v = (y << 25) | ((long) month << 21) | ((long) day << 16) |
      ((long) hour << 11) | ((long) minute << 5) | ((long) (second >> 1))
    return v & 0xFFFFFFFFL
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------ the fields

  public void setSize(long v) {
    size = v
  }

  public long getSize() {
    return size
  }

  /* Setting this is what tells ZipOutputStream that the caller knows the
     compressed size -- and therefore that the entry can be written with its
     sizes in the local header instead of a data descriptor. An entry that came
     from a reader has a compressed size and has not been through here, which is
     exactly Java's distinction and why it is a separate bit rather than a test
     against -1. */
  public void setCompressedSize(long v) {
    csize = v
    csizeSet = true
  }

  public long getCompressedSize() {
    return csize
  }

  public void setCrc(long v) {
    crc = v
  }

  public long getCrc() {
    return crc
  }

  /* Java keeps setMethod and getMethod package-private; here they are the only
     way a caller can say which of the two methods an entry uses. The value is
     checked where java.util.zip checks it, so an entry cannot be made to name a
     method the writer would then have to refuse. */
  public void setMethod(int m) {
    if (m != STORED && m != DEFLATED) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("invalid compression method")
    }
    method = m
  }

  public int getMethod() {
    return method
  }

  public void setExtra(byte[] b) {
    /* The only thing java.util.zip checks here: the field's own length, which
       is a 16-bit number in both headers. A malformed extra field is left for a
       reader to walk, as Java leaves it. */
    if (b != null && b.length > 0xFFFF) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("invalid extra field length")
    }
    extra = b
  }

  public byte[] getExtra() {
    return extra
  }

  public void setComment(String c) {
    comment = c
  }

  public String getComment() {
    return comment
  }

  /* Java's definition: an entry whose name ends in '/' is a directory, and that
     is the whole of it -- the format has no directory bit. */
  public boolean isDirectory() {
    return name.endsWith("/")
  }

  public String toString() {
    return name
  }

  public int hashCode() {
    return name.hashCode()
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the fields

/* The little-endian field readers the two parsers share.

   Everything in a ZIP header is little-endian and nothing else in this library
   is -- DataInputStream reads big-endian, which is Java's byte order and not
   the archive's -- so the two readers that parse a header out of a byte array
   share these two calls rather than each carrying its own shift-and-mask. The
   writer has no counterpart here: it writes through ZipOutputStream's own
   helpers, which count the bytes they write as they go. */
final class ZipFields {
  public static int get16(byte[] b, int at) {
    return (b[at] & 0xFF) | ((b[at + 1] & 0xFF) << 8)
  }

  public static long get32(byte[] b, int at) {
    return (long) (b[at] & 0xFF) |
      ((long) (b[at + 1] & 0xFF) << 8) |
      ((long) (b[at + 2] & 0xFF) << 16) |
      ((long) (b[at + 3] & 0xFF) << 24)
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- writing

/* java.util.zip.ZipOutputStream: an archive, written once, in order.

   The stream keeps what the central directory needs -- every entry and the
   offset of its local header -- and writes nothing but the local headers and
   the data until finish, which is what makes it a stream: an entry's bytes do
   not have to be known when the entry before it is written. */
class ZipOutputStream extends Object implements OutputStream {
  public static final int STORED = ZipEntry.STORED
  public static final int DEFLATED = ZipEntry.DEFLATED

  /* Java's default buffer: the size of the array the deflater is drained
     through, not a buffer of the stream's own. */
  private static final int DEFAULT_BUFSIZE = 512

  /* The one flag this class decides: the sizes are not in the local header. */
  private static final int DESCRIPTOR_FLAG = 8
  /* The language encoding flag, which every entry here carries. */
  private static final int UTF8_FLAG = 0x800

  private static final long LOCSIG = 0x04034b50L
  private static final long EXTSIG = 0x08074b50L
  private static final long CENSIG = 0x02014b50L
  private static final long ENDSIG = 0x06054b50L

  /* The value a 32-bit field holds when the real one is in a ZIP64 record, and
     the value a 16-bit entry count holds when the same is true of it. Both are
     refused rather than written. */
  private static final long ZIP64_MAGIC = 0xFFFFFFFFL
  private static final int ZIP64_COUNT = 0xFFFF

  private OutputStream out
  private Deflater deflater
  private CRC32 crc = new CRC32()
  private byte[] buf
  /* Every byte written to out, which is what makes a local header's offset a
     running total rather than a count of entries. */
  private long written = 0
  private byte[] comment = null
  private int method = DEFLATED

  /* The central directory, in the order the entries were added: the entries and
     the offset of each local header, in parallel arrays that grow together. */
  private ZipEntry[] entries = new ZipEntry[8]
  private long[] offsets = new long[8]
  private int count = 0

  /* Java refuses two entries with one name, because a reader that looks an
     entry up by name would find one of them and an extractor that writes them
     out would overwrite. */
  private HashSet<String> names = new HashSet<String>()

  private ZipEntry current = null
  /* What the current entry has produced so far: the bytes the caller wrote and
     the bytes the deflater emitted. The header is written before either is
     known, so these are what the verification and the data descriptor use. */
  private long entryIn = 0
  private long entryOut = 0
  private boolean finished = false
  private boolean closed = false
  private byte[] one = new byte[1]

  public ZipOutputStream(OutputStream out) {
    this(out, DEFAULT_BUFSIZE)
  }

  public ZipOutputStream(OutputStream out, int size) {
    if (out == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("out")
    }
    this.out = out
    this.buf = new byte[size > 0 ? size : DEFAULT_BUFSIZE]
    /* A raw deflate stream: the ZIP format has no wrapper of its own, and the
       zlib header gzip's cousin carries would be eight bytes of nothing in
       front of every entry. */
    this.deflater = new Deflater(Deflater.DEFAULT_COMPRESSION, true)
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------ the knobs

  /* Java's IllegalArgumentException for a comment the field cannot hold, checked
     here rather than at finish time so that a caller learns at the call. */
  public void setComment(String c) {
    if (c == null) {
      comment = null
      return
    }
    byte[] bytes = c.getBytes()
    if (bytes.length > 0xFFFF) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("ZIP file comment too long")
    }
    comment = bytes
  }

  /* The method an entry uses when it did not choose one. */
  public void setMethod(int m) {
    if (m != DEFLATED && m != STORED) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("invalid compression method")
    }
    method = m
  }

  public void setLevel(int level) {
    deflater.setLevel(level)
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------ the entries

  public void putNextEntry(ZipEntry e) {
    ensureOpen()
    if (e == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("entry")
    }
    if (current != null) {
      closeEntry()
    }
    if (e.dosTime == -1) {
      /* Java's default, and it is what makes an entry written without a time
         carry the time it was written at. */
      e.setTime(System.currentTimeMillis())
    }
    if (e.method == -1) {
      e.method = method
    }
    e.flag = 0
    if (e.method == DEFLATED) {
      if (e.size == -1 || e.csize == -1 || e.crc == -1 || e.csizeSet == false) {
        e.flag = DESCRIPTOR_FLAG
      }
    } else if (e.method == STORED) {
      if (e.size == -1) {
        e.size = e.csize
      } else if (e.csize == -1) {
        e.csize = e.size
      } else if (e.size != e.csize) {
        throw new ZipException("STORED entry where compressed != uncompressed size")
      }
      if (e.size == -1 || e.crc == -1) {
        throw new ZipException("STORED entry missing size, compressed size, or crc-32")
      }
    } else {
      throw new ZipException("unsupported compression method")
    }
    if (names.add(e.name) == false) {
      throw new ZipException("duplicate entry: " + e.name)
    }
    e.flag = e.flag | UTF8_FLAG
    current = e
    entryIn = 0
    entryOut = 0
    addEntry(e, written)
    writeLOC(e)
  }

  public void closeEntry() {
    ensureOpen()
    if (current == null) {
      return
    }
    ZipEntry e = current
    if (e.method == DEFLATED) {
      deflater.finish()
      while (deflater.finished() == false) {
        drain()
      }
      if ((e.flag & DESCRIPTOR_FLAG) == 0) {
        /* The caller said what the sizes would be; this is where a wrong answer
           is caught, which is the whole value of writing them up front. */
        if (e.size != entryIn) {
          throw new ZipException("invalid entry size (expected " + e.size +
            " but got " + entryIn + " bytes)")
        }
        if (e.csize != entryOut) {
          throw new ZipException("invalid entry compressed size (expected " +
            e.csize + " but got " + entryOut + " bytes)")
        }
        if (e.crc != crc.getValue()) {
          throw new ZipException("invalid entry CRC-32 (expected 0x" +
            Long.toHexString(e.crc) + " but got 0x" + Long.toHexString(crc.getValue()) + ")")
        }
      } else {
        /* The descriptor is filled from what the compressor actually did, and
           the entry is updated with it so that a caller can read back what it
           could not know in advance. */
        e.size = entryIn
        e.csize = entryOut
        e.crc = crc.getValue()
        writeEXT(e)
      }
    } else if (e.method == STORED) {
      if (e.size != entryIn) {
        throw new ZipException("invalid entry size (expected " + e.size +
          " but got " + entryIn + " bytes)")
      }
      if (e.crc != crc.getValue()) {
        throw new ZipException("invalid entry crc-32 (expected 0x" +
          Long.toHexString(e.crc) + " but got 0x" + Long.toHexString(crc.getValue()) + ")")
      }
    } else {
      throw new ZipException("invalid compression method")
    }
    crc.reset()
    deflater.reset()
    current = null
  }

  /* The central directory and the end record. closing the stream calls this,
     and a caller who finishes an archive and then writes another file to the
     same stream does so through it. */
  public void finish() {
    ensureOpen()
    if (finished) {
      return
    }
    if (current != null) {
      closeEntry()
    }
    if (count > ZIP64_COUNT - 1) {
      throw new ZipException("too many entries in ZIP file for an archive without ZIP64: " + count)
    }
    long off = written
    for (int i = 0 : i < count : i++) {
      writeCEN(entries[i], offsets[i])
    }
    writeEND(off, written - off)
    finished = true
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------ the writes

  public void write(int b) {
    one[0] = (byte) b
    write(one, 0, 1)
  }

  public void write(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    if (b == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException()
    }
    if (off < 0 || len < 0 || off > b.length - len) {
      throw new ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException("Range [" + off + ", " + off +
        " + " + len + ") out of bounds for length " + b.length)
    }
    if (len == 0) {
      return
    }
    if (current == null) {
      throw new ZipException("no current ZIP entry")
    }
    ZipEntry e = current
    if (e.method == DEFLATED) {
      crc.update(b, off, len)
      entryIn += len
      deflater.setInput(b, off, len)
      while (deflater.needsInput() == false) {
        drain()
      }
    } else if (e.method == STORED) {
      /* A STORED entry's local header has already promised its size, so a write
         past it is a header that would be a lie -- Java's message, and its
         check, which is the only way to keep the promise true. */
      if (entryIn + len > e.size) {
        throw new ZipException("attempt to write past end of STORED entry")
      }
      out.write(b, off, len)
      written += len
      entryIn += len
      crc.update(b, off, len)
    } else {
      throw new ZipException("invalid compression method")
    }
  }

  /* What the deflater has ready, pushed to the stream. A compressed entry is
     written as it is compressed -- nothing here holds an entry's bytes -- so
     this is the call that makes the class a stream rather than a buffer. */
  private void drain() {
    while (true) {
      int n = deflater.deflate(buf, 0, buf.length, Deflater.NO_FLUSH)
      if (n > 0) {
        out.write(buf, 0, n)
        written += n
        entryOut += n
      }
      if (n < buf.length) {
        return
      }
    }
  }

  /* Java's flush, which is DeflaterOutputStream's with syncFlush false: what the
     stream under this one holds goes out, and the tail of the entry being
     compressed stays where it is, because the deflater cannot end the block
     without ending the entry. */
  public void flush() {
    out.flush()
  }

  public void close() {
    if (closed) {
      return
    }
    finish()
    closed = true
    deflater.end()
    out.close()
  }

  private void ensureOpen() {
    if (closed) {
      throw new IOException("Stream closed")
    }
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------ the records

  /* How many bytes the entry needs before a reader can find its data: 20 for a
     DEFLATED entry, because that is the first version of the format that had
     deflate, and 10 for a STORED one. Java answers the same two numbers, and a
     reader is entitled to believe them. */
  private int version(ZipEntry e) {
    if (e.method == DEFLATED) {
      return 20
    }
    if (e.method == STORED) {
      return 10
    }
    throw new ZipException("unsupported compression method")
  }

  /* A value that has to fit a 32-bit little-endian field. A negative one means
     a caller set a size the entry never reached, which is a different bug with
     the same answer: the field cannot hold it. */
  private long field(long v, String what) {
    if (v < 0 || v >= ZIP64_MAGIC) {
      throw new ZipException(what + " does not fit a ZIP field without ZIP64: " + v)
    }
    return v
  }

  private void writeLOC(ZipEntry e) {
    writeInt(LOCSIG)
    writeShort(version(e))
    writeShort(e.flag)
    writeShort(e.method)
    writeInt(e.dosTime)
    if ((e.flag & DESCRIPTOR_FLAG) != 0) {
      writeInt(0)
      writeInt(0)
      writeInt(0)
    } else {
      writeInt(e.crc)
      writeInt(field(e.csize, "compressed size"))
      writeInt(field(e.size, "size"))
    }
    byte[] nameBytes = e.name.getBytes()
    if (nameBytes.length > 0xFFFF) {
      throw new ZipException("entry name too long")
    }
    int elen = e.extra == null ? 0 : e.extra.length
    if (elen > 0xFFFF) {
      throw new ZipException("entry extra data too long")
    }
    writeShort(nameBytes.length)
    writeShort(elen)
    writeBytes(nameBytes, 0, nameBytes.length)
    if (elen > 0) {
      writeBytes(e.extra, 0, elen)
    }
  }

  /* The data descriptor, which a reader finds after the compressed data rather
     than before it. The signature is optional in the format and written here
     because the JDK writes it and because without it a reader has to guess
     whether the first four bytes are a CRC-32 or the signature. */
  private void writeEXT(ZipEntry e) {
    writeInt(EXTSIG)
    writeInt(e.crc)
    writeInt(field(e.csize, "compressed size"))
    writeInt(field(e.size, "size"))
  }

  /* The entry's second description, at the end of the archive, where a reader
     that can seek looks first. Everything a reader needs is here except the
     data: the sizes, the CRC-32, the offset of the local header, and the name,
     extra field and comment again. */
  private void writeCEN(ZipEntry e, long offset) {
    byte[] nameBytes = e.name.getBytes()
    if (nameBytes.length > 0xFFFF) {
      throw new ZipException("entry name too long")
    }
    int elen = e.extra == null ? 0 : e.extra.length
    if (elen > 0xFFFF) {
      throw new ZipException("entry extra data too long")
    }
    byte[] commentBytes = e.comment == null ? null : e.comment.getBytes()
    int clen = commentBytes == null ? 0 : commentBytes.length
    if (clen > 0xFFFF) {
      throw new ZipException("entry comment too long")
    }
    writeInt(CENSIG)
    /* Version made by and version needed are the same number here: this writer
       is not a UNIX tool and records no file attributes, so the high byte of
       version-made-by, which is the making system, is not written. The external
       attributes field below is zero for the same reason -- the ZIP format
       keeps UNIX permissions there, and this library's entries have none. */
    writeShort(version(e))
    writeShort(version(e))
    writeShort(e.flag)
    writeShort(e.method)
    writeInt(e.dosTime)
    writeInt(e.crc)
    writeInt(field(e.csize, "compressed size"))
    writeInt(field(e.size, "size"))
    writeShort(nameBytes.length)
    writeShort(elen)
    writeShort(clen)
    writeShort(0)
    writeShort(0)
    writeInt(0)
    writeInt(field(offset, "local header offset"))
    writeBytes(nameBytes, 0, nameBytes.length)
    if (elen > 0) {
      writeBytes(e.extra, 0, elen)
    }
    if (clen > 0) {
      writeBytes(commentBytes, 0, clen)
    }
  }

  private void writeEND(long off, long len) {
    writeInt(ENDSIG)
    writeShort(0)
    writeShort(0)
    writeShort(count)
    writeShort(count)
    writeInt(field(len, "central directory size"))
    writeInt(field(off, "central directory offset"))
    if (comment == null) {
      writeShort(0)
    } else {
      writeShort(comment.length)
      writeBytes(comment, 0, comment.length)
    }
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------ the fields

  /* Every one of these counts what it wrote, because the offset of the next
     local header is exactly what has been written so far. */
  private void writeShort(int v) {
    out.write(v & 0xFF)
    out.write((v >>> 8) & 0xFF)
    written += 2
  }

  private void writeInt(long v) {
    out.write((int) (v & 0xFF))
    out.write((int) ((v >>> 8) & 0xFF))
    out.write((int) ((v >>> 16) & 0xFF))
    out.write((int) ((v >>> 24) & 0xFF))
    written += 4
  }

  private void writeBytes(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    out.write(b, off, len)
    written += len
  }

  private void addEntry(ZipEntry e, long offset) {
    if (count == entries.length) {
      ZipEntry[] bigger = new ZipEntry[entries.length * 2]
      long[] wider = new long[offsets.length * 2]
      System.arraycopy(entries, 0, bigger, 0, count)
      System.arraycopy(offsets, 0, wider, 0, count)
      entries = bigger
      offsets = wider
    }
    entries[count] = e
    offsets[count] = offset
    count = count + 1
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- reading

/* java.util.zip.ZipInputStream: an archive read from front to back, one entry
   at a time, without seeking.

   This is the reader for a stream that cannot go back: an HTTP body, a pipe, a
   file whose local headers are enough. It reads the local headers in order and
   trusts them, which is what makes it cheap and what makes its answers weaker
   than ZipFile's when a header disagrees with the central directory. The end of
   the archive is the first four bytes that are not a local header -- for a
   complete archive, the central directory's own signature -- and that is a null
   from getNextEntry, not an error.

   The reading state is one byte buffer and a pending region, as in
   GZIPInputStream: the inflater is fed from the buffer and consumes only part
   of it, so the bytes it did not consume are where the data descriptor and the
   next local header start. getRemaining is the inflater's own count of them,
   and the pending region is recomputed from it whenever an entry ends.

   Java's InputStream grew readAllBytes, readNBytes, skipNBytes, skip and
   transferTo over the releases, and this class inherits none of them: nothing
   in this library's stream hierarchy declares a method of that family, and the
   one place in this file that reads a whole entry (ZipFile.getInputStream's
   callers, and the tests) reads in a loop. */
class ZipInputStream extends InputStream implements Reader {
  private static final int DEFAULT_BUFSIZE = 512
  private static final int DESCRIPTOR_FLAG = 8
  private static final long ZIP64_MAGIC = 0xFFFFFFFFL
  private static final long LOCSIG = 0x04034b50L
  private static final long EXTSIG = 0x08074b50L
  private static final int LOCHDR = 30

  private InputStream in
  private byte[] buf
  private Inflater inflater = new Inflater(true)
  private CRC32 crc = new CRC32()
  private ZipEntry entry = null
  /* The local header on its way in, reused rather than allocated per entry. */
  private byte[] header = new byte[LOCHDR]
  /* Bytes read from the stream that the inflater has not consumed. inLen is the
     length of the last chunk fed to it, so the pending region is the tail of
     that chunk, which is what getRemaining counts from. */
  private int pendingPos = 0
  private int pendingEnd = 0
  private int inLen = 0
  private long remaining = 0
  private boolean entryEOF = false
  private boolean closed = false
  private byte[] one = new byte[1]
  private byte[] drainBuf = new byte[256]
  private LineScanner scanner = null

  public ZipInputStream(InputStream in) {
    this(in, DEFAULT_BUFSIZE)
  }

  public ZipInputStream(InputStream in, int size) {
    if (in == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("in")
    }
    this.in = in
    this.buf = new byte[size > 0 ? size : DEFAULT_BUFSIZE]
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------ the entries

  /* The next local header, or null at the end of the archive. Closing the entry
     before it is what makes this read the data the previous one left, so a
     caller who reads every entry never needs closeEntry. */
  public ZipEntry getNextEntry() {
    ensureOpen()
    if (entry != null) {
      closeEntry()
    }
    crc.reset()
    inflater.reset()
    /* The pending region is NOT reset: the next header starts inside the bytes
       the inflater did not consume, and throwing them away would lose it. */
    entry = readLOC()
    if (entry == null) {
      return null
    }
    if (entry.method == ZipEntry.STORED) {
      remaining = entry.size
    }
    entryEOF = false
    return entry
  }

  /* Reads the current entry to its end, checking what the header promised. A
     caller who does not want the data pays for reading it, which is what a
     stream that cannot seek has to do. */
  public void closeEntry() {
    ensureOpen()
    while (read(drainBuf, 0, drainBuf.length) != -1) {
    }
    entryEOF = true
  }

  public int available() {
    return entryEOF ? 0 : 1
  }

  private ZipEntry readLOC() {
    int got = 0
    while (got < LOCHDR) {
      int b = nextByte()
      if (b < 0) {
        /* The archive ended inside what would have been a header. Java answers
           the same thing: fewer than thirty bytes left is the end. */
        return null
      }
      header[got] = (byte) b
      got = got + 1
    }
    if (ZipFields.get32(header, 0) != LOCSIG) {
      /* Not a local header: for a complete archive this is the central
         directory's signature, which is where this reader stops. */
      return null
    }
    int flag = ZipFields.get16(header, 6)
    int method = ZipFields.get16(header, 8)
    long time = ZipFields.get32(header, 10)
    int nlen = ZipFields.get16(header, 26)
    int elen = ZipFields.get16(header, 28)
    byte[] nameBytes = new byte[nlen]
    readFully(nameBytes, 0, nlen)
    ZipEntry e = new ZipEntry(Net.stringFrom(nameBytes, 0, nlen))
    e.flag = flag
    e.method = method
    e.dosTime = time
    if ((flag & 1) != 0) {
      throw new ZipException("encrypted ZIP entry not supported")
    }
    if ((flag & DESCRIPTOR_FLAG) != 0) {
      /* A data descriptor is only defined for a deflated entry: for a stored one
         there is no way to know where the data ends, so a reader cannot honour
         the flag. Java refuses it by name. */
      if (method != ZipEntry.DEFLATED) {
        throw new ZipException("only DEFLATED entries can have EXT descriptor")
      }
      e.size = -1
      e.csize = -1
      e.crc = -1
    } else {
      e.crc = ZipFields.get32(header, 14)
      e.csize = ZipFields.get32(header, 18)
      e.size = ZipFields.get32(header, 22)
      if (e.csize == ZIP64_MAGIC || e.size == ZIP64_MAGIC) {
        throw new ZipException("ZIP64 entries are not implemented")
      }
    }
    if (elen > 0) {
      byte[] extra = new byte[elen]
      readFully(extra, 0, elen)
      e.extra = extra
    }
    return e
  }

  /* The end of a deflated entry: the sizes the header could not carry, if a
     data descriptor is what follows the data, and then the three checks. Java
     makes the same three, in the same order and with the same messages. */
  private void readEnd(ZipEntry e) {
    pendingEnd = inLen
    pendingPos = inLen - (int) inflater.getRemaining()
    if ((e.flag & DESCRIPTOR_FLAG) != 0) {
      long sig = nextUInt()
      long c = sig
      if (sig == EXTSIG) {
        c = nextUInt()
      }
      e.crc = c
      e.csize = nextUInt()
      e.size = nextUInt()
      if (e.csize == ZIP64_MAGIC || e.size == ZIP64_MAGIC) {
        throw new ZipException("ZIP64 entries are not implemented")
      }
    }
    if (e.size != inflater.getTotalOut()) {
      throw new ZipException("invalid entry size (expected " + e.size +
        " but got " + inflater.getTotalOut() + " bytes)")
    }
    if (e.csize != inflater.getTotalIn()) {
      throw new ZipException("invalid entry compressed size (expected " + e.csize +
        " but got " + inflater.getTotalIn() + " bytes)")
    }
    if (e.crc != crc.getValue()) {
      throw new ZipException("invalid entry CRC (expected 0x" + Long.toHexString(e.crc) +
        " but got 0x" + Long.toHexString(crc.getValue()) + ")")
    }
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------ the reads

  public int read() {
    int n = read(one, 0, 1)
    if (n < 0) {
      return -1
    }
    return one[0] & 0xFF
  }

  /* Java's odd contract, kept: this returns -1 at the END OF THE CURRENT ENTRY
     rather than at the end of the stream, so a caller reads one entry, takes
     the -1, and asks for the next entry. */
  public int read(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    ensureOpen()
    if (b == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException()
    }
    if (off < 0 || len < 0 || off > b.length - len) {
      throw new ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException("Range [" + off + ", " + off +
        " + " + len + ") out of bounds for length " + b.length)
    }
    if (len == 0) {
      return 0
    }
    if (entry == null) {
      return -1
    }
    ZipEntry e = entry
    if (e.method == ZipEntry.DEFLATED) {
      while (true) {
        if (inflater.finished()) {
          readEnd(e)
          entry = null
          entryEOF = true
          return -1
        }
        if (inflater.needsInput()) {
          fill()
        }
        int n = inflater.inflate(b, off, len)
        if (n > 0) {
          crc.update(b, off, n)
          return n
        }
      }
    } else if (e.method == ZipEntry.STORED) {
      if (remaining <= 0) {
        entry = null
        entryEOF = true
        return -1
      }
      if (len > remaining) {
        len = (int) remaining
      }
      int n = readRaw(b, off, len)
      if (n < 0) {
        throw new ZipException("unexpected EOF")
      }
      crc.update(b, off, n)
      remaining = remaining - n
      if (remaining == 0 && e.crc != crc.getValue()) {
        throw new ZipException("invalid entry CRC (expected 0x" + Long.toHexString(e.crc) +
          " but got 0x" + Long.toHexString(crc.getValue()) + ")")
      }
      return n
    } else {
      throw new ZipException("invalid compression method")
    }
  }

  /* The prelude's readln reads the console, which a stream over an archive must
     not do. */
  public String readln() {
    if (scanner == null) {
      scanner = new LineScanner(this)
    }
    return scanner.readLine()
  }

  public void close() {
    if (closed) {
      return
    }
    closed = true
    entry = null
    inflater.end()
    if (in instanceof AutoCloseable) {
      ((AutoCloseable) in).close()
    }
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------ the input

  /* More bytes for the inflater: the ones it did not consume first, then the
     stream. Reading them moves the pending region to the front of the buffer,
     which is what keeps inLen and getRemaining counting from the same place. */
  private void fill() {
    if (pendingPos < pendingEnd) {
      int n = pendingEnd - pendingPos
      System.arraycopy(buf, pendingPos, buf, 0, n)
      pendingPos = 0
      pendingEnd = 0
      inLen = n
      inflater.setInput(buf, 0, n)
      return
    }
    if (in instanceof ByteArrayInputStream) {
      int n = ((ByteArrayInputStream) in).read(buf, 0, buf.length)
      if (n <= 0) {
        throw new EOFException("Unexpected end of ZLIB input stream")
      }
      inLen = n
      inflater.setInput(buf, 0, n)
      return
    }
    /* One byte at a time, for the reason GZIPInputStream documents in 44_zip:
       this library's InputStream declares read() and readln() and nothing
       else, so the only read every stream must answer is the single-byte one. */
    int b = in.read()
    if (b < 0) {
      throw new EOFException("Unexpected end of ZLIB input stream")
    }
    buf[0] = (byte) b
    inLen = 1
    inflater.setInput(buf, 0, 1)
  }

  /* One byte, from the pending region first and then from the stream, or -1 at
     the end of the stream. */
  private int nextByte() {
    if (pendingPos < pendingEnd) {
      int v = buf[pendingPos] & 0xFF
      pendingPos = pendingPos + 1
      return v
    }
    return in.read()
  }

  /* A block read that takes the pending region first: the bytes the inflater
     did not consume come before anything still in the stream, and a STORED
     entry's data can begin inside them. */
  private int readRaw(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    if (pendingPos < pendingEnd) {
      int n = pendingEnd - pendingPos
      if (n > len) {
        n = len
      }
      System.arraycopy(buf, pendingPos, b, off, n)
      pendingPos = pendingPos + n
      return n
    }
    if (in instanceof ByteArrayInputStream) {
      return ((ByteArrayInputStream) in).read(b, off, len)
    }
    int v = in.read()
    if (v < 0) {
      return -1
    }
    b[off] = (byte) v
    return 1
  }

  private void readFully(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    while (len > 0) {
      int n = readRaw(b, off, len)
      if (n <= 0) {
        throw new EOFException()
      }
      off = off + n
      len = len - n
    }
  }

  private long nextUInt() {
    long v = 0
    for (int i = 0 : i < 4 : i++) {
      int b = nextByte()
      if (b < 0) {
        throw new EOFException()
      }
      v = v | ((long) b << (8 * i))
    }
    return v
  }

  private void ensureOpen() {
    if (closed) {
      throw new IOException("Stream closed")
    }
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- a file

/* An Enumeration over a snapshot of the central directory, which is what
   java.util.zip.entries() returns.

   The JDK's enumeration reads the central directory as it walks, so a call
   after the archive has been closed is an IllegalStateException; this one walks
   an array built when the archive was opened, so closing it does not stop an
   enumeration that was already handed out. That is the difference a reader that
   has the whole archive in memory buys, and it is the only one. */
final class ZipEntryEnumeration implements Enumeration<ZipEntry> {
  private final ZipEntry[] entries
  private final int count
  private int at = 0

  public ZipEntryEnumeration(ZipEntry[] entries, int count) {
    this.entries = entries
    this.count = count
  }

  public boolean hasMoreElements() {
    return at < count
  }

  public ZipEntry nextElement() {
    if (at >= count) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException()
    }
    ZipEntry e = entries[at]
    at = at + 1
    return e
  }
}

/* The stream over one entry of an already-open archive: the bytes of a STORED
   entry as they are, or the inflated bytes of a DEFLATED one.

   It exists because ZipFile has the whole archive in memory and can hand out a
   slice of it, where ZipInputStream has to read forward through everything
   before the entry. The bytes it serves are bounded by the compressed size the
   central directory gave, so a corrupt length cannot make it read into the next
   entry, and it checks the CRC-32 and the size when the entry's data runs out
   (see the note at the top of this file about that check and the JDK). */
class ZipEntryStream extends InputStream implements Reader {
  private byte[] data
  private int pos
  private int end
  private Inflater inflater = null
  private CRC32 crc = new CRC32()
  private int method
  private long size
  private long expectedCrc
  private long served = 0
  private boolean eof = false
  private boolean closed = false
  private byte[] one = new byte[1]
  private LineScanner scanner = null

  public ZipEntryStream(byte[] data, int off, int len, int method,
                        long size, long expectedCrc) {
    this.data = data
    this.pos = off
    this.end = off + len
    this.method = method
    this.size = size
    this.expectedCrc = expectedCrc
    if (method == ZipEntry.DEFLATED) {
      this.inflater = new Inflater(true)
      this.inflater.setInput(data, off, len)
    } else if (method != ZipEntry.STORED) {
      throw new ZipException("invalid compression method")
    }
  }

  public int read() {
    int n = read(one, 0, 1)
    if (n < 0) {
      return -1
    }
    return one[0] & 0xFF
  }

  public int read(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    if (b == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException()
    }
    if (off < 0 || len < 0 || off > b.length - len) {
      throw new ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException("Range [" + off + ", " + off +
        " + " + len + ") out of bounds for length " + b.length)
    }
    if (len == 0) {
      return 0
    }
    if (closed) {
      throw new IOException("Stream closed")
    }
    if (eof) {
      return -1
    }
    if (method == ZipEntry.STORED) {
      if (pos >= end) {
        check()
        eof = true
        return -1
      }
      int n = end - pos
      if (n > len) {
        n = len
      }
      System.arraycopy(data, pos, b, off, n)
      pos = pos + n
      crc.update(b, off, n)
      served = served + n
      return n
    }
    while (true) {
      if (inflater.finished()) {
        check()
        eof = true
        return -1
      }
      if (inflater.needsInput()) {
        /* The whole compressed entry was handed to the inflater in the
           constructor, so needing input means the bytes the central directory
           promised are all there were: the entry is truncated, and there is no
           next entry to read into. Java's message for the same situation. */
        throw new EOFException("Unexpected end of ZLIB input stream")
      }
      int n = inflater.inflate(b, off, len)
      if (n > 0) {
        crc.update(b, off, n)
        return n
      }
    }
  }

  /* What the central directory said, against what came out. */
  private void check() {
    long produced = method == ZipEntry.DEFLATED ? inflater.getTotalOut() : served
    if (produced != size) {
      throw new ZipException("invalid entry size (expected " + size +
        " but got " + produced + " bytes)")
    }
    if (crc.getValue() != expectedCrc) {
      throw new ZipException("invalid entry CRC (expected 0x" + Long.toHexString(expectedCrc) +
        " but got 0x" + Long.toHexString(crc.getValue()) + ")")
    }
  }

  public String readln() {
    if (scanner == null) {
      scanner = new LineScanner(this)
    }
    return scanner.readLine()
  }

  public void close() {
    if (closed) {
      return
    }
    closed = true
    if (inflater != null) {
      inflater.end()
    }
  }
}

/* java.util.zip.ZipFile: an archive read at random.

   The whole archive is read into memory and the central directory is parsed
   from its end, which is where a reader that can look everywhere starts: the
   end record is at a fixed distance from the end, the central directory is at
   the offset it gives, and every entry's local header is at an offset the
   central directory gives. Nothing walks the local headers, so an archive whose
   local headers disagree with its central directory is read the way its central
   directory describes it.

   Java's ZipFile reads through a seekable channel and one constructor takes a
   Charset; here the archive is a byte array (this library has no seekable byte
   channel, and a file, a jar and a response body are all byte arrays already),
   the name encoding is UTF-8 always, and ZipFile(byte[]) is the constructor
   java.util.zip does not have. All the rest of the surface is Java's:
   entries(), getEntry, getInputStream, size(), getComment, getName, close. What
   is not here is Java 8's stream() and the OPEN_READ/OPEN_DELETE mode
   constants: there is no handle to open, only bytes that have already arrived,
   and entries() is the whole of the iteration. A path that is not there is also
   not the JDK's answer: the bytes are read by Files.readAllBytes, so a missing
   file is its NoSuchFileException rather than java.io.FileNotFoundException. */
class ZipFile extends Object implements AutoCloseable {
  private static final long ENDSIG = 0x06054b50L
  private static final long CENSIG = 0x02014b50L
  private static final long LOCSIG = 0x04034b50L
  private static final int ENDHDR = 22
  private static final int CENHDR = 46
  private static final int LOCHDR = 30
  /* How far back the end record can be: its own 22 bytes and the longest
     comment the format allows. */
  private static final int MAX_COMMENT = 0xFFFF
  private static final long ZIP64_MAGIC = 0xFFFFFFFFL
  private static final int ZIP64_COUNT = 0xFFFF

  private byte[] data
  private String path
  private String comment = null
  private ZipEntry[] entries = null
  private long[] offsets = null
  private int count = 0
  private HashMap<String, Integer> byName = new HashMap<String, Integer>()
  private boolean closed = false

  public ZipFile(String name) {
    if (name == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("name")
    }
    this.path = name
    this.data = Files.readAllBytes(name)
    readCentralDirectory()
  }

  public ZipFile(File file) {
    if (file == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("file")
    }
    this.path = file.getPath()
    this.data = Files.readAllBytes(this.path)
    readCentralDirectory()
  }

  /* An archive that is already bytes -- a jar's body, an HTTP response -- with
     no file behind it. getName answers "" for this one, because there is no
     path to answer with. */
  public ZipFile(byte[] bytes) {
    if (bytes == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("bytes")
    }
    this.path = ""
    this.data = bytes
    readCentralDirectory()
  }

  public String getName() {
    return path
  }

  public String getComment() {
    return comment
  }

  public int size() {
    ensureOpen()
    return count
  }

  public Enumeration<ZipEntry> entries() {
    ensureOpen()
    return new ZipEntryEnumeration(entries, count)
  }

  /* The entry with this name, or null. Java looks for the name and then, if the
     name was not there, for the directory entry with a slash on the end, which
     is why getEntry("dir") finds the entry named "dir/". */
  public ZipEntry getEntry(String name) {
    if (name == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("name")
    }
    ensureOpen()
    Integer at = byName.get(name)
    if (at == null) {
      at = byName.get(name + "/")
      if (at == null) {
        return null
      }
    }
    return entries[at.intValue()]
  }

  /* A stream over the entry's data, or null when the entry is not in this
     archive -- Java's answer for an entry from somewhere else, and the reason
     this looks the entry up again by name rather than trusting the object.

     The local header is read here, in the archive's bytes, to find where the
     data starts: a local header's extra field is allowed to differ in length
     from the central directory's, so the offset the central directory gives is
     the header's, not the data's.

     The answer is a ZipEntryStream rather than the InputStream java.util.zip
     declares, and for the reason GZIPInputStream documents in 44_zip: this
     library's InputStream declares read() and readln() and no block read, so a
     caller handed the interface could only read an archive one byte at a time.
     The concrete class is still an InputStream, so it goes anywhere one is
     wanted. */
  public ZipEntryStream getInputStream(ZipEntry entry) {
    if (entry == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("entry")
    }
    ensureOpen()
    Integer at = byName.get(entry.name)
    if (at == null) {
      return null
    }
    int index = at.intValue()
    long off = offsets[index]
    if (off < 0 || off + LOCHDR > data.length) {
      throw new ZipException("ZipFile invalid LOC header (bad signature)")
    }
    if (ZipFields.get32(data, (int) off) != LOCSIG) {
      throw new ZipException("ZipFile invalid LOC header (bad signature)")
    }
    int nlen = ZipFields.get16(data, (int) off + 26)
    int elen = ZipFields.get16(data, (int) off + 28)
    int start = (int) off + LOCHDR + nlen + elen
    ZipEntry e = entries[index]
    if (start + e.csize > data.length) {
      throw new ZipException("ZipFile error reading zip file")
    }
    return new ZipEntryStream(data, start, (int) e.csize, e.method, e.size, e.crc)
  }

  public void close() {
    closed = true
  }

  private void ensureOpen() {
    if (closed) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("zip file closed")
    }
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------ the reading

  /* The end record, then the central directory, then the index by name. */
  private void readCentralDirectory() {
    int len = data.length
    /* Java's check, which is on the length being zero rather than on it being
       shorter than an end record: a shorter file is searched like any other and
       answers "no end record" rather than "empty". */
    if (len == 0) {
      throw new ZipException("zip file is empty")
    }
    int at = findEnd(len)
    long total = ZipFields.get16(data, at + 10)
    long cdSize = ZipFields.get32(data, at + 12)
    long cdOff = ZipFields.get32(data, at + 16)
    int comlen = ZipFields.get16(data, at + 20)
    if (cdSize == ZIP64_MAGIC || cdOff == ZIP64_MAGIC || total == ZIP64_COUNT) {
      throw new ZipException("ZIP64 archives are not implemented")
    }
    if (cdOff + cdSize > len) {
      throw new ZipException("invalid END header (bad central directory size)")
    }
    if (comlen > 0) {
      comment = Net.stringFrom(data, at + ENDHDR, comlen)
    }
    int limit = (int) (cdOff + cdSize)
    /* The number of entries the records hold, counted before they are read: the
       end record's own count is not trusted, which is what the JDK does with it
       as well -- it walks the records and takes what it finds, so a header that
       says three and holds five is read as five. */
    int real = countRecords((int) cdOff, limit)
    entries = new ZipEntry[real]
    offsets = new long[real]
    int pos = (int) cdOff
    while (pos + CENHDR <= limit) {
      if (ZipFields.get32(data, pos) != CENSIG) {
        throw new ZipException("invalid CEN header (bad signature)")
      }
      int flag = ZipFields.get16(data, pos + 8)
      int method = ZipFields.get16(data, pos + 10)
      long time = ZipFields.get32(data, pos + 12)
      long crc = ZipFields.get32(data, pos + 16)
      long csize = ZipFields.get32(data, pos + 20)
      long size = ZipFields.get32(data, pos + 24)
      int nlen = ZipFields.get16(data, pos + 28)
      int elen = ZipFields.get16(data, pos + 30)
      int clen = ZipFields.get16(data, pos + 32)
      long off = ZipFields.get32(data, pos + 42)
      int nameAt = pos + CENHDR
      if (nameAt + nlen + elen + clen > data.length) {
        throw new ZipException("invalid CEN header (bad header size)")
      }
      if ((flag & 1) != 0) {
        throw new ZipException("invalid CEN header (encrypted entry)")
      }
      if (method != ZipEntry.DEFLATED && method != ZipEntry.STORED) {
        throw new ZipException("invalid CEN header (bad compression method: " + method + ")")
      }
      if (csize == ZIP64_MAGIC || size == ZIP64_MAGIC || off == ZIP64_MAGIC) {
        throw new ZipException("ZIP64 entries are not implemented")
      }
      String name = Net.stringFrom(data, nameAt, nlen)
      ZipEntry e = new ZipEntry(name)
      e.flag = flag
      e.method = method
      e.dosTime = time
      e.crc = crc
      e.csize = csize
      e.size = size
      if (elen > 0) {
        byte[] extra = new byte[elen]
        System.arraycopy(data, nameAt + nlen, extra, 0, elen)
        e.extra = extra
      }
      if (clen > 0) {
        e.comment = Net.stringFrom(data, nameAt + nlen + elen, clen)
      }
      entries[count] = e
      offsets[count] = off
      /* Two entries may share a name -- the format allows it even though this
         writer refuses to make one -- and the first is the one a lookup finds,
         which is the order Java's central-directory search walks. */
      if (byName.get(name) == null) {
        byName.put(name, count)
      }
      count = count + 1
      pos = nameAt + nlen + elen + clen
    }
  }

  /* The records walked without being interpreted: a record's length is its own
     header plus the three length fields in it, so the count is a walk rather
     than a read, and a record whose lengths do not land inside the central
     directory is the one thing that walk can say. */
  private int countRecords(int at, int limit) {
    int n = 0
    int pos = at
    while (pos + CENHDR <= limit) {
      int nlen = ZipFields.get16(data, pos + 28)
      int elen = ZipFields.get16(data, pos + 30)
      int clen = ZipFields.get16(data, pos + 32)
      int next = pos + CENHDR + nlen + elen + clen
      if (next > limit) {
        throw new ZipException("invalid CEN header (bad header size)")
      }
      pos = next
      n = n + 1
    }
    if (pos != limit) {
      throw new ZipException("invalid CEN header (bad header size)")
    }
    return n
  }

  /* The end record is found by looking for its signature from the end backwards
     rather than forwards from the front, because its position is the one thing
     about the archive that is not in a field: a comment of any length up to
     65535 bytes may follow it, and a reader that does not know the length has to
     search. The last signature that is followed by exactly a comment's worth of
     bytes is the record. */
  private int findEnd(int len) {
    int lowest = len - ENDHDR - MAX_COMMENT
    if (lowest < 0) {
      lowest = 0
    }
    for (int at = len - ENDHDR : at >= lowest : at--) {
      if (ZipFields.get32(data, at) == ENDSIG) {
        int comlen = ZipFields.get16(data, at + 20)
        if (at + ENDHDR + comlen == len) {
          return at
        }
      }
    }
    throw new ZipException("zip END header not found")
  }
}
